      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To review the current literature and guidelines for pediatric and adolescent human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) diagnosis and treatment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
          "content": "MEDLINE and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Web sites were searched to obtain relevant articles."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY SELECTION",
          "content": "The expert opinions of the authors were used to design cases that highlight the studies and guidelines reviewed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There have been numerous advances in the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of pediatric and adolescent HIV disease in the past decade. Now, HIV is a chronic illness managed with medication, when appropriate. The HIV transmission rate from mother to child is very low (approximately 1%) in industrialized countries because antiretroviral agents are now available to pregnant women. The pill burden for children and adults with HIV has plummeted from 8 to 10 pills 3 times daily to 3 to 5 pills once daily in the best-case scenarios. A shift has occurred in HIV care from treating opportunistic illnesses to promoting adherence and avoiding drug resistance while monitoring medication adverse effects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Diagnosis and treatment of HIV is a rapidly changing field with frequently changing guidelines. The coming years may bring already available technology to the rest of the world and advances toward the prevention and cure of HIV."
        }
      ]},
